```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a downed VLAN 10 sub-interface on mswitch1, the distribution-layer
switch that bridges switch1 (where PC4 and PC5 live) to router0 (which hosts the
172.16.10.1 gateway). The topology uses per-VLAN Linux bridge instances: br_vlan10
and br_vlan20, each fed by 802.1Q sub-interfaces on the trunk links. On mswitch1,
the sub-interface eth0.10 — the VLAN 10 logical member on the eth0 trunk toward
switch1 — is administratively DOWN (kernel flags show <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> with no
UP bit, state DOWN). The peer sub-interfaces are all healthy: eth0.20 on the same
physical link is UP (which is why laptop0 on VLAN 20 sees zero impact), and
eth4.10 toward router0 is also UP. The gateway itself (router0.eth0.10 =
172.16.10.1/24) is UP and properly configured. Because eth0.10 on mswitch1 is
down, Layer 2 frames from PC4/PC5 flowing through switch1's br_vlan10 arrive at
mswitch1's physical eth0 port but the VLAN 10 sub-interface cannot receive them,
so they never enter mswitch1's br_vlan10 bridge and are dropped — resulting in
"Destination Host Unreachable" ARP failures before even reaching the gateway.

Root cause: mswitch1.eth0.10 has been administratively shut down, severing the
VLAN 10 L2 segment between switch1 and the distribution core.

Corrective fix: On mswitch1, run `ip link set eth0.10 up` to restore the
sub-interface. To make the fix persistent across reboots, the startup script for
mswitch1 must also include this command (or remove any `ip link set eth0.10 down`
directive that was injected).
=== END ===
```